=== REASONING ===

ADR-015 invariants the slice touches and how each is preserved:

- I1 default-off:    Registry entry sets `DefaultOn: false`. The TS mirror exposes the same flag; the SPA
                     `useAiEnabled('ml-charging-curve-clustering')` hook returns false until the user opts
                     in. No code path silently flips it on.
- I3 baseline intact: The deterministic Charging Curve page at /charging/curves keeps rendering the
                     existing rule-label charts + per-session label heuristic; the ML3 component is an
                     opt-in NARRATIVE adjacent block. The trainer is read-only against `charging_sessions`
                     and never persists envelopes — every render is a fresh request-scoped train.
- I4 zero egress:    Redaction policy is `PolicyChatbot` (deny-all). The strategy never emits provider
                     egress for non-narrative tool results; the canned mock provider is the only provider
                     wired up in CI.
- I5 hidden UI:      The AI section is wrapped by `withAiFeature('ml-charging-curve-clustering', ...)` which
                     returns `null` when `useAiEnabled` is false. The off-mode vitest proves no DOM markers
                     leak.
- I6 404 routes:     `POST /api/v1/ai/ml/charging-curves/cluster` is registered behind `g.Wrap` at
                     internal/api/ai_routes.go (in the `/ml/charging-curves/cluster` block). The Go off-mode
                     test exercises the 404 path; the off-mode SPA test exercises the absent-DOM path.
- I7 per-feature:    The two gates (mode != 'off' AND per-feature toggle on) are AND-of-both-paths;
                     defence-in-depth case in the off-mode test pins this. Sibling distinction case proves
                     the C3 toggle does NOT enable ML3.
- I10 type system:   Feature ID, route paths, JobNames, PushKinds, UITestIDs are reflected from
                     `internal/ai/features/registry.go` to `web/src/ai/features.ts` via `tools/aigen` in the
                     same commit; aivet enforces the mirror is in sync.
- I12 client/bg:     This slice ships no jobs, no push events, no service-worker chunks, no client
                     storage. JobNames / PushKinds in the registry are forward-compat declarations only so
                     the off-mode coverage walker can prove their absence today; no scheduler entry, no SW
                     manifest entry, no localStorage key.

Why mirror slice 0063 (ML2 range-prediction-model) and not slice 0028 (C3 charging-curve-fingerprint-
clustering): the methodology tier ML3 maps to a *learned statistical envelope*, not a *narrator over an
existing aggregator*. The trainer must produce per-cluster envelopes (mean / stddev / p5 / p95 per
cluster), with explicit fallback labels when fewer than `mlchargingcurves.DefaultMinSessionsPerCluster=3`
sessions exist. Slice 0063 already established the pattern (trainer in `internal/ml/`, two READ-only
tools `train_*` + `query_*`, strategy + canned goldens, handler + parity test, frontend wired component
+ off-mode + on-mode tests). The slice 0028 narrator is a sibling that consumes a different aggregator
output; it is intentionally NOT touched by this slice.

Parity test rationale: `internal/ml/chargingcurves.ClassifyChargingPowerTier` MUST agree with the C3
sibling's `internal/ai/tools/charge_curve_clustering.classifyChargingPowerTier` at every representative
power point (boundary points: 0, 1920, 1921, 19200, 19201; interior points: 1500, 7000, 50000, 250000;
edge case: nil / negative). A future change that updates one classifier without mirroring the change to
the other would silently produce contradicting narrations between the two surfaces on the same page;
this test is the load-bearing guard that keeps the duplication safe. The duplication itself is documented
in `internal/ml/chargingcurves/clustering.go`'s docstring.

Tools naming: `RegisterChargeCurveClustersTools` (plural) because the underlying tool name is
`train_charge_curve_clusters` and `query_charge_curve_clusters` (clusters plural). Backend route is
`cluster` (singular) per slice prompt — this is intentional (the route is a verb: "perform clustering",
returning a single training run; the tools' return value is "a set of clusters").

